WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.849 --> 00:00:06.470
So comrades, welcome to the lecture. What
I am going to do

00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:12.360
now, we’re going to begin with an experiment
to illustrate

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:20.250
this point that the oxygen molecule, O2, has
unpaired

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:21.250
electrons.

00:00:21.250 --> 00:00:23.340
Now we’re outside just to show you the reactivity
of this

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:26.800
lovely stuff here, liquid oxygen. So you can
see wonderful

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:31.260
liquid oxygen is a nice blue colour. So over
here on the table

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:36.530
we’ve got a tin tray which we’re going
to use to contain the

00:00:36.530 --> 00:00:38.739
experiment that I’m going to show you next.
So we’ve got

00:00:38.739 --> 00:00:44.089
our favourite liquid oxygen. Strongly oxidising;
very, very

00:00:44.089 --> 00:00:46.920
reactive, and here I’ve got some cotton
wool. So this is the

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:51.269
same stuff we use to take make-up off, perhaps
our partners

00:00:51.269 --> 00:00:53.429
or our girlfriends or our wives, and this
is the same stuff we

00:00:53.429 --> 00:00:55.889
use to wipe our babies’ bums.

00:00:55.889 --> 00:00:58.370
Oxygen is an interesting element because it
exists in two

00:00:58.370 --> 00:01:03.339
forms: the taught form which we breathe all
the time is O2,

00:01:03.339 --> 00:01:07.380
two oxygen atoms joined together; and there’s
another form

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:12.170
called ozone, and which has three atoms arranged
like the

00:01:12.170 --> 00:01:16.350
letter V, or letter V this way up.

00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:19.890
Oxygen has unpaired electrons within the molecular
orbitals,

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:22.840
in the atomic orbitals around the structure,
and these

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:26.130
unpaired electrons give rise to colour and
that colour is blue.

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:29.619
So blue is often perceived to be the colour
of solvated or

00:01:29.619 --> 00:01:32.659
unpaired electrons, and you can see here the
lovely blue

00:01:32.659 --> 00:01:35.610
colour. Now lots of chemists are really scared
when they see

00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:39.009
the blue of liquid oxygen, because liquid
oxygen is very, very

00:01:39.009 --> 00:01:42.590
reactive. Generally you only make liquid oxygen
by mistake.

00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:45.380
Not like today when we’re making some on
purpose. And if

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:48.439
you’ve made it by mistake in an uncontrolled
way, where

00:01:48.439 --> 00:01:50.950
there might be organic molecules perhaps simple
organic

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:54.310
chemicals like hydrocarbons, grease or perhaps
a highly

00:01:54.310 --> 00:01:57.249
elaborate compound then, they tend to detonate
and they

00:01:57.249 --> 00:01:59.700
tend to cause very energetic experiments.

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:03.069
Yeah it’s very fluffy it’s not very reactive.
It doesn’t really

00:02:03.069 --> 00:02:06.840
burn, it cinders, might smoulder. But here
we go, we’re going

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:08.039
to pop it in the tray.

00:02:08.039 --> 00:02:13.300
So we begin with Neil’s very high-tech piece
of equipment,

00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:16.820
which is a test-tube on a piece of string.
What we’re going to

00:02:16.820 --> 00:02:24.890
do is to put some liquid nitrogen in here.
You can see liquid

00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:30.740
nitrogen – well you may not see – is colourless.
Ok? So this is

00:02:30.740 --> 00:02:35.600
liquid nitrogen, and what I hope you can see
is that this

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:40.420
magnet, which is a pretty good magnet. Let
me just show

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:46.150
you here with my keys, that they stick pretty
strongly to the

00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:52.150
magnet. The magnet has absolutely no affect
at all on the

00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:55.990
liquid nitrogen. I can pull the magnet and
nothing happens.

00:02:55.990 --> 00:02:58.370
And then we’re going to fill up all of the
void space, so all of

00:02:58.370 --> 00:03:03.010
the space in the cotton particles with oxygen.
So you might

00:03:03.010 --> 00:03:05.990
want to stand back after this bit Brady. So
here we go, here’s

00:03:05.990 --> 00:03:09.650
our liquid oxygen. Do you want to come in
and zoom in on

00:03:09.650 --> 00:03:10.650
me?

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:12.170
It’s ok, I’ve got it zoomed nicely.

00:03:12.170 --> 00:03:15.440
Ok, so here you can see the lovely blue liquid
oxygen colour,

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:17.930
going into the cotton wool particles.

00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:27.070
Let’s try the same thing with liquid oxygen.
Careful of my

00:03:27.070 --> 00:03:28.130
shoes…

00:03:28.130 --> 00:03:30.950
So here we have a match on a stick, and I
think you can see

00:03:30.950 --> 00:03:34.380
its probably alight, so we have our fire,
we have our oxygen,

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:35.771
and we have our fuel. Now let’s see what
happens when we

00:03:35.771 --> 00:03:37.370
put them all together.

00:03:37.370 --> 00:03:43.430
The liquid oxygen is a very nice blue colour
today. So now

00:03:43.430 --> 00:03:48.900
let’s try, here’s the magnet again, and
if I take the liquid

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:55.220
oxygen you can see I can pull it right up
here. It’s not as

00:03:55.220 --> 00:03:58.900
magnetic as my keys, but you can see there’s
a really big

00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:03.980
difference. It sticks to the magnet. So you
can see it really is

00:04:03.980 --> 00:04:05.490
magnetic.

00:04:05.490 --> 00:04:15.920
You ready? Ok. Woohoo and there she blows!

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:21.260
And ozone is much more reactive than oxygen.
So near the

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:25.720
earth’s surface, where you and I are at
the moment, ozone is

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:28.510
really quite dangerous because if you breathe
it in it can start

00:04:28.510 --> 00:04:34.000
reacting with any sort of molecule that has
bonds between

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:39.020
carbon. But in the upper atmosphere, ozone
is extremely

00:04:39.020 --> 00:04:42.910
important because it absorbs ultra-violet
light that comes

00:04:42.910 --> 00:04:47.970
from the sun and stops this ultra-violet light
attacking the

00:04:47.970 --> 00:04:51.970
molecules in biological species on the surface.
If it wasn’t for

00:04:51.970 --> 00:04:57.340
the ozone in the upper atmosphere all of us
would be, if not

00:04:57.340 --> 00:05:01.010
dead, certainly very much less comfortable
than we are at the

00:05:01.010 --> 00:05:02.010
moment.

00:05:02.010 --> 00:05:05.060
So Neil’s now connecting up a piece of tube
to oxygen

00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:08.070
cylinder and he is going to fill up this gas
jar with oxygen and

00:05:08.070 --> 00:05:10.560
we are going to do a really classic experiment:
the one that

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:13.180
everyone learns at school and tests for oxygen,
which is to

00:05:13.180 --> 00:05:19.490
relight a glowing splint. So we have a traditional
gas jar full of

00:05:19.490 --> 00:05:24.070
oxygen. So here you can see we are burning
a splint. Ok, so

00:05:24.070 --> 00:05:29.450
this is the wood burning in excess oxygen
around us, ok. So

00:05:29.450 --> 00:05:32.820
what we are going to do is we are going to
take the splint out

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:35.531
so it is just glowing. And if I blow on this
splint you can see it

00:05:35.531 --> 00:05:40.300
gets brighter. Now we are going to try and
relight that splint

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:43.120
by putting it into a very oxygen-rich atmosphere.
So we’ll

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:47.080
move over to the jar and we’ll put in our
glowing splint and

00:05:47.080 --> 00:05:50.639
you can see the flame comes back to life.
So that’s a test that

00:05:50.639 --> 00:05:53.550
everyone learns for an oxygen-rich environment.

00:05:53.550 --> 00:05:54.930
What happened? What made that happen?

00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:58.960
Well the increased oxygen content/concentration
reignites and

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:02.080
reinitiates that oxidation of the wood or
the burning of the

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:08.460
wood. So we’ll do that again. So there we
go: flame again.

